<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="A4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="A4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hyperparameter Sensitivity Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct sensitivity analyses for two key hyperparameters in <span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Secret</span> to understand their impact on performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of Local Query Budget (<math id="A4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{local}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">local</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{local}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</span>
The Local Exploitation (LE) phase (Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Appendix III Omitted Algorithms ‣ External Data Extraction Attacks against Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) uses a local query budget, denoted here as <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{local}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>local</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{local}}</annotation></semantics></math>, determining the maximum number of queries spent exploring a single cluster before attempting another Global Jump (GE). To assess sensitivity, we vary <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{local}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>local</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{local}}</annotation></semantics></math> within a reasonable range [10, 100] and measure the resulting MER on the HealthcareMagic-101 dataset using the GPT-4o mini as backend LLM. The results are plotted in Figure <a href="#A4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix IV Omitted Experimental Results ‣ External Data Extraction Attacks against Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The curve shows that the overall MER remains relatively stable across the tested range, which indicates that <span id="A4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Secret</span> is not overly sensitive to the exact value of <math id="A4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\text{local}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mtext>local</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\text{local}}</annotation></semantics></math> within typical operational ranges, suggesting that a moderate budget (<math id="A4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e.g." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e.g.</annotation></semantics></math>, 30, as used in our main experiments) is sufficient.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE VIII: </span>Effectiveness comparison of different attacks against naive defenses. The dataset is Enron Mail.</figcaption>
